Subject: [PATCH 10737/11362] The headline levels count is a final-frame delta
 and disagrees with the per-level record

The cd82/r11l run finished cleanly at 07:41Z, rc=0. r11l scored 2 levels in 2121
actions; cd82 scored 0 in 2222. The cd82 row is the finding.

Its own fields contradict each other. levels 0, reached 0, deepest_level_reached 0,
alongside level_up_charged [771], per_level[0] completed true with 741 agent
actions, and per_level[1] with 1481 agent actions. The agent completed level 0 and
then spent 1481 actions inside level 1, and the headline says it never left level 0.

The mechanism is arc_leaderboard_eval.py:757. reached is the level of the FINAL
frame and levels is that minus the start, so an agent that completes a level and is
later reset back scores zero for it. cd82 has 82 nav resets and
resets_before_levelups [30], consistent with ending below its deepest point.

The supervisor receipt was right and I doubted it. cd82's redirects carry
resolved_by_levelup true with actions_to_levelup 651, 531 and 411 -- three pending
redirects credited by the single level-up at 771, which level_up_charged confirms.
The receipt agrees with the per-level record and the headline is the outlier.

Consequences: the run printed LEADERBOARD SCORE 2 levels where completed levels give
3, and the dashboard generalization line moved 7 to 5, so a reset-after-completion
reads as regression against the prior cd82 run that scored 2.

The entry deliberately does not pick a side. Either levels should count completed
levels and the delta undercounts resets, or it measures the level held at the end
and a reset forfeits credit. Which is right depends on what the scored Kaggle metric
does with a reset, and that was not checked. deepest_level_reached 0 is hard to
defend either way, since the agent demonstrably reached level 1.

A method note is recorded with it. Within twenty minutes I claimed the two receipts
were byte-identical, and that a helped credit with zero levels was impossible. Both
were wrong and both came from reading summary counters instead of the redirects
list. The counters coincide across games because the supervisor fires at fixed
action indices.

